course: everyday
lesson: 516
title: Divorce and two homes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When parents divorce or separate, they stop being a couple, but they never stop being parents. Ruby-Lou has two homes: Mum's flat on school days and Dad's house at weekends. It's Monday, it's PE, and her kit is in a heap on Dad's bedroom floor. «Again?!» says Mrs Bell. Ruby-Lou goes red. That night Dad helps her write a checklist (PE kit, reading book, inhaler, charger) and buys a bright yellow travel bag that goes wherever she goes. She still forgets things sometimes, but much less.
explain: The night before Dad moved out, Ashley had a row with him about her bedtime. For weeks she thought, «It's because of me.» In the end she said it out loud, and Mum sat on her bed: «Grown-ups split up for grown-up reasons, petal. Never because of a bedtime. Not you, never you.» Dad said exactly the same on the phone. Divorce is never the child's fault, even when it feels like it.
explain: Two homes often mean two sets of rules. At Dad's, Ruaridh gets pizza and stays up till ten. At Mum's it's veg and lights out at half eight. «Dad's house is way better, it's bussin',» he says, and Mum's face drops. «Bussin'?» says Gran. «That means good, does it? Well, Dad's is a holiday, love. Mum's is the everyday.» Hester has two rooms: Da's has fairy lights, Mam's is plain. She feels bad for liking Da's more, until she tells Mam. Mam laughs, and they buy fairy lights for her room too.
explain: Some things are just hard. Jamie-Leigh goes to Dad's in Edinburgh every other weekend: an hour on the train each way. «Aw, it's a pure trek,» she groans; in Scotland «pure» can mean really. She tells them, and now on some Sundays Dad comes to her instead. At every handover Tanner's mum and dad snipe at each other (little sharp, mean remarks), and Tanner stares at the floor. One day he says, «Can yous not do that in front of me?» («yous» = you lot). Some weeks they manage. Some weeks they don't. Not everything gets fixed.
explain: Leo-James's mum has a boyfriend, Craig, who says, «Shoes off, sunshine, my rules.» Leo-James shouts, «You're not my dad!» Mum tells Leo he's right, and that he can say it without shouting; Craig backs off and stops giving orders. Jory hears the words «custody hearing» and «the judge will decide», and lies awake scared he'll have to pick Mum or Dad. A kind lady from the court comes to ask what he thinks: children are listened to, but they don't have to choose; the grown-ups decide. And sometimes the end is gentle: Avery's mum and dad sit side by side at her school play. «They're mates now,» Avery tells everyone. Frida gets Christmas at Mum's and Boxing Day at Dad's. «Double presents!» says her pal. «Double car journeys, more like,» says Frida, grinning anyway.
fact: Divorce or separation means parents stop being a couple; they are still both the child's parents.
fact: Divorce is never the child's fault; parents split up for grown-up reasons.
fact: Many children live in two homes, for example school days with one parent and weekends with the other.
fact: A checklist and a travel bag help children stop leaving things at the other home.
fact: Two homes can have different rules; comparing them out loud can hurt a parent.
fact: It is fine to like things about each home, and you can tell a parent what you like.
fact: Long journeys between homes are tiring; children can say so, and plans can sometimes change.
fact: Parents arguing at handovers puts a child in the middle; a child may ask them to stop, though it doesn't always work.
fact: A parent's new partner is not a replacement parent, and it takes time to get used to them.
fact: When parents can't agree, a family court may decide where children live; children are listened to but do not have to choose.
fact: Some separated parents become friends, and some never get on.
fact: Two Christmases can be fun and tiring at the same time.
fact: In Scotland «pure» can mean really, «snipe» means make sharp mean remarks, «bussin'» is young slang for really good, and «yous» means you lot.
exercise: Ruby-Lou's PE kit is at Dad's on a Monday again. What helped her? | A checklist and a travel bag that goes with her | Asking Mrs Bell to move PE to a day she is at Mum's | Keeping no PE kit at all, so there's nothing to forget | Living only at Mum's house from now on, whatever happens
exercise: Why did Ashley think the divorce was her fault? | She'd had a row with Dad about bedtime | She'd broken Dad's favourite mug that week | Her teacher had told her so in front of the class | Her big brother kept saying it to her every day
exercise: Whose fault is a divorce? | Never the child's | The child's, if there was a row | The youngest child's | Whoever the judge picks
exercise: «Dad's house is way better, it's bussin'.» What does «bussin'» mean? | Really good | Really busy | Full of buses | Really boring
exercise: Why did Mum's face drop when Ruaridh compared the houses? | Comparing homes out loud can hurt a parent | She wanted to order pizza for herself too | She was cross he'd used a slang word | She hadn't known Dad had his own house at all
exercise: What did Gran mean by «Dad's is a holiday, Mum's is the everyday»? | Treats at one house don't make it the better home | Dad's house is abroad, somewhere sunny with a beach | Ruaridh should only ever visit Dad in the holidays | Mum's house is boring and he should move to Dad's
exercise: Hester felt bad for loving Da's fairy lights. What did she do? | Told Mam, and they got fairy lights for her room too | Kept it secret so Mam would never ever find out | Took the fairy lights from Da's room without asking | Stopped going to Da's at all because it felt so unfair to Mam
exercise: «Aw, it's a pure trek.» What does Jamie-Leigh mean? | The trip is really long and tiring | The train is very clean and new | It's a nice walk in the hills | It's a short, easy hop on a fast train
exercise: What changed after Jamie-Leigh said the journey was too much? | On some Sundays Dad comes to her instead | She stopped seeing her dad altogether | Her dad moved in next door to her mum | She now has to go every single weekend
exercise: At handovers Tanner's parents «snipe». What is sniping? | Making sharp, mean little remarks | Hugging and saying a kind goodbye | Shouting at the child to hurry | Swapping bags without a word
exercise: What did Tanner ask his parents? | Not to do it in front of him | To get back together right away | To let him pick one house for ever | To snipe at each other more quietly
exercise: Did Tanner's parents stop sniping for good? | Some weeks they managed, some weeks not | Yes, and they never sniped again | Yes, they got back together that week | No, they started sniping at Tanner
exercise: What is true about a parent's new partner, like Craig? | He isn't a replacement dad, and it takes time | He must be called Dad from the very first day | His rules count for more than Mum's rules do | He should never be spoken to at all, ever
exercise: Jory hears «the judge will decide». What does a family court do? | Listens to children, then the grown-ups decide | Makes the child point at one parent in court | Sends a parent to prison for divorcing | Decides who was naughtiest in the family
exercise: Does Jory have to choose between Mum and Dad? | No | Yes, by pointing at one of them in court | Yes, by writing a letter to the judge | Only if he's the oldest child in the family
exercise: Avery's parents sit together at her play. What does that show? | Some separated parents stay friends | They have just got married again | All separated parents hate each other | They are only allowed to meet at plays
exercise: «Double presents!» says Frida's pal. What does Frida answer? | «Double car journeys, more like» | «Double homework, more like» | «I only get one present» | «I don't like Christmas any more»
test: «my mate says if ur parents divorce its cos u were bad». Right? | No, it's never the child's fault | Yes, if you were naughty that week | Yes, kids always cause it somehow | No, it's always the grandparents' fault
test: Ruby-Lou forgets her kit, Mrs Bell: «Again?!». What would most likely happen if she checked her list every Sunday night? | She'd forget things less often | Mrs Bell would stop PE for the whole class | Dad would move into Mum's flat to save time | She would never need a PE kit again
test: «sod it, i'm not going to Dad's, it's a pure trek». What's a better move? | Tell her parents the trip wears her out | Hide in the toilets so she misses the train | Never speak to Dad again because of a train | Pretend to be ill every other Friday from now on
test: In a cartoon, a clever kid makes her divorced parents fall back in love. What is more real? | Kids can't fix it; some parents end up friends and some don't | Every divorced couple gets back together in the end | Any kid with a clever enough plan can reunite them | Parents who divorce never speak to each other again
test: Tanner mutters, «yous are always at it». Who is he talking to? | Both his parents | Only his mum | Only his dad | His teacher and the class
test: Leo-James shouts «You're not my dad!» What would be an honest and calmer way to say it? | «You're not my dad, so Mum makes the rules» | «Get out of our house right now, you idiot» | «Fine then, I'll call you Dad from today» | Saying nothing at all and slamming every door
test: Jory: «if the judge makes me pick I'll be sick». What's true? | He'll be listened to, but he won't have to pick | The judge will toss a coin to pick for him | He must choose out loud with both parents there | Children are never asked anything about it
test: Ruaridh: «Dad's is better cos he lets me stay up». What would most likely happen if he said that to Mum every week? | Mum would keep feeling hurt | Mum would start letting him stay up till ten | Dad would make his rules strict as well | Nothing, parents never mind being compared
test: Hester's mate: «u can't like one house more, that's well disloyal». Right? | No, it's fine to like things about each home | Yes, liking one more means you love that parent more | Yes, and you must never say which bits you like | No, but you have to pick one and stay there
test: ★★★ Frida spends Christmas Day at Mum's and Boxing Day at Dad's. Next year they swap the two days round. Where is she on Christmas Day next year? | At Dad's | At Mum's | At both at once | At Gran's
test: ★★★ Hester sleeps at Mam's Monday to Thursday and at Da's Friday to Sunday. The fairy lights are at Da's. How many nights a week does she fall asleep under them? | 3 | 4 | 7 | 2
test: Avery says her parents are «mates now». What would most likely happen at her next birthday? | Both could come without a row | Neither parent would come at all | They'd argue all through the cake | She'd need two parties on one day
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cause of a parental divorce? | It arises from adult reasons and is never attributable to the child | It is ordinarily attributable to the conduct of the eldest child | It follows from disputes concerning a child's bedtime | It is determined by the court to be the fault of a child
exam: Given that a child resides in two households, identify the measure most likely to prevent belongings being left at the other residence. | A checklist and a bag that accompanies the child between homes | The duplication of every possession at both residences | The cancellation of activities requiring equipment | The permanent removal of the child to a single home
exam: Ascertain the role of the child where a family court is required to determine with which parent the child shall reside. | The child's views are heard, whereas the decision rests with adults | The child is obliged to nominate one parent in open court | The child is excluded from any expression of views whatsoever | The child determines the matter alone by written declaration
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a parent's new partner. | He or she does not replace the parent, and acceptance takes time | He or she assumes all parental authority upon arrival | He or she must be addressed as a parent from the first day | He or she must not be addressed by the child at all, in any circumstances
